1. Volatile Process Hashing (SHA-256)

Attackers frequently perform process masquerading (e.g., naming a malicious binary svchost.exe and running it from a non-standard path like C:\Users\Public). WinFAE records the path of every active process and automatically computes its SHA-256 hash.

DFIR Value: These hashes can be queried automatically against the VirusTotal API or an organization's threat-intel baseline database to isolate malicious binaries instantly.

2. Live Socket Connection Mapping

Established TCP/UDP sockets are matched directly to their corresponding Process IDs (PIDs) and Process Names.

DFIR Value: This allows immediate isolation of malicious Command and Control (C2) beacons or active reverse shells bypasses by validating whether a generic process (like notepad.exe) is holding an active external connection.

3. Registry & Task Scheduler Persistence Audit

Malware often modifies standard autorun nodes or schedule engines to survive system restarts. WinFAE inspects:

HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run

HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run

Non-Microsoft Scheduled Tasks (filtering out native system noise)

DFIR Value: Isolates persistence vectors commonly leveraged by APT threat actors to establish long-term footholds inside environments.

🚀 How to Run the Tool

Prerequisites

Windows 10/11 or Windows Server 2016/2019/2022.

Elevated Administrator PowerShell Console.

Execution

Download the tool to the target machine via a secure USB or network share:

git clone https://github.com/yourusername/WinFAE.git
cd WinFAE


Set the Execution Policy bypass to execute the local script:

Set-ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Scope Process -Force


Run the extractor specifying an output directory:

.\Get-ForensicArtifacts.ps1 -OutputDir "C:\Triage_Output"


